data protection regulation topped  discussions on friday 13th during the  second and last day of the justice and  home affairs council in brussels  eu justice ministers reached a partial  general approach on some issues linked  to data protection  they expressed their support for the  creation of a one-stop shop mechanism  that will play a role in important  cross-border cases  it will also provide for cooperation and  joint decision making between data  protection authorities  this system would allow citizens to  launch complaints with regulators in  their country of residence  the latvian justice minister welcomed  the progress made during a news  conference after chairing the meeting  thanks to the one-stop agency principal  that is  fundamentally new in data protection  persons are insured  protection of their rights within easy  reach  in case of infringement  citizens will have  a possibility to contact  an authority of their country  data protection in the european union is  a fundamental right  that is why its legislative reform seeks  to ensure a very high level of  protection of personal data
 

